# Case Summary Format (Section 1 of RE)

**Goal:** Anyone skimming section 1 must instantly know **what is wrong** and **what the customer wants** — nothing else.

## Length (strict)

- **Target: 35–50 words.** Hard **max 60** words (even with multiple issues: compress, do not list everything).
- If you exceed 60 words, you failed the format — rewrite shorter.

## What to put (in this order, plain language)

1. **Fall # + customer name** (once, short).
2. **The actual problem** — one concrete sentence (wrong bill, missing delivery, login fails, cancellation not processed, etc.). No vague “issue” or “concern”.
3. **What they want now** — one short phrase (refund, fix, confirmation, transfer, etc.).
4. **Prior o2/brand** — **optional, max one short clause** only if it changes understanding (e.g. “o2 already sent duplicate invoice”). If nothing material, **omit**.
5. **Date context** — one short clause if a deadline or date matters (past/future). Otherwise **omit**.

## Cut out (mandatory)

- Thread play-by-play, chronology, “first they wrote then we…”
- Emotions, apologies, “customer feels”, “unfortunately”
- Explaining Sprinklr, process, or internal steps
- Repeating subject line or long quotes from the email
- Secondary issues unless they are the same problem in one phrase
- Anything that does not help a human decide **case type + urgency + ask** in 5 seconds

## Style

- **One short paragraph.** No bullets. No “The customer stated…”.
- Prefer **subject–verb–object** sentences over abstract nouns.
- Bad: *“There have been ongoing difficulties regarding billing alignment post-migration.”*
- Good: *“Double charge on March invoice; customer wants correction.”*

## Micro-template (mental)

`Fall #____, Name: [concrete problem]. Wants: [ask]. [Optional: one prior o2 fact.] [Optional: date note.]`

Apply to **every** RE section 1.
